HOW TO DECIDE
1. The target role is the role name of the posting without seniority words (Senior, Staff, Lead, Principal), team, product or location. Example: the headline "Backend Engineer - Payments Core" gives the target role "Backend Engineer".
2. If the title already contains the target role as a phrase, leave it: status same.
3. A title may take the wording of the target role when the candidate's work in that place is of that kind. Adjacent specialisations are interchangeable: AI, LLM, ML, GenAI, Generative AI, Applied AI, Machine Learning in front of Engineer or Developer. Software, Backend, Python, Platform and Full-Stack before Engineer or Developer are allowed when the work text shows that kind of work. Examples: "LLM Engineer" for the target role "AI Engineer" becomes "AI Engineer". "AI Engineer" for the target role "Backend Engineer", with a work text about building backend services, becomes "Backend Engineer".
4. Role nouns that the candidate has never held (Manager, Lead, Head, Director, Designer, Analyst, Scientist, Architect, Consultant, Recruiter, Salesperson) are never written as a role. Roles that already stand in the candidate's own titles (Founder, Engineer, Developer) may be kept and reordered.
5. When the target role is a profession the candidate never held but the work text shows part of its function, add the function in parentheses after the existing title, using the words of the work text. Example: target role "Product Manager", the work text says "wrote a business plan and technical/product roadmap": "Founder and AI Engineer (product and roadmap ownership)". Never write the target role name, and never write a role noun, inside the parentheses. If the work text shows no such function, leave the title unchanged and say so in the reason.
6. A title that joins two roles: put the role that matches the target first in the target wording, then " & ", then the other role exactly as it was. Example: "Founder and ML Engineer" for the target role "AI Engineer" becomes "AI Engineer & Founder".
7. kind="main" is the headline under the candidate's name. Keep the name, the separators and the punctuation, and apply the same rules to the role words. If the headline joins two specialisations with a slash and one of them equals the target role, write the target role and keep the other after " / ". Example: "AI/LLM Engineer" for the target role "AI Engineer" becomes "AI Engineer / LLM".
8. Never add seniority words, never change company names or dates. If the title text contains the company name or other words around the role, keep them exactly and change only the role words.
9. Follow the candidate's note when there is one.
10. If nothing fits, status same, with a reason of at most 10 words that says why (for example "different profession" or "no such work in the description").

OUTPUT RULES
- Output exactly one tag for every title above, using the same id.
- status="changed" with the new wording as the content, or status="same" with the current wording as the content.
- reason: at most 10 words. For changed it says what in the work text supports the new wording. For same it says why nothing changed.
- Never use a double quote or the characters < and > inside a reason. Plain text only.

OUTPUT FORMAT
<title_suggestion id="0" status="changed" reason="{what supports it}">{new wording}</title_suggestion>
<title_suggestion id="1" status="same" reason="{why unchanged}">{current wording}</title_suggestion>